{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a prince of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was still a child when his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent for him until he came of age.\n\nAfter his mother's regency ended Raymond III began to take part in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and the two men were close. In 1164 Raymond was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the Battle of Harim. He was held captive for nearly ten years and was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid. *Reign* After his release Raymond returned to Tripoli and began to consolidate his power. He married Eschiva of Bures a widow and a relative of the lord of Tiberias.\n\nRaymond was a skilled politician and military leader. He played an important role in the politics of the Crusader states and was often called upon to act as regent for other nobles. In 1174 he was regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was a minor. *Succession Crisis and Death* Raymond's regency ended when Baldwin came of age but he continued to play an important role in the politics of the Crusader states. In 1187 Raymond was one of the leaders of the Crusader army at the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster and Raymond was one of the few leaders who escaped. He died soon after the battle possibly due to injuries or illness. Raymond had no children and the county of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV. Raymond III was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to shape the politics of the region for many years after his death.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.018178174272179604}, {"token": "mond", "logpro